---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/omnisend-vs-shopify-email';
const title = 'Omnisend vs Shopify Email (now Messaging) for Shopify campaigns';
const desc = 'Compare Omnisend with Shopify Messaging for eligible email and SMS campaigns, automations, purchase exits and a controlled campaign pilot.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Shopify Email support SMS now?",
    "answer": "Shopify Email is now Shopify Messaging. It supports email and eligible SMS campaigns and automations; check store access, sender registration and supported recipient countries before planning a send."
  },
  {
    "question": "When is Omnisend a good fit?",
    "answer": "Consider Omnisend when one team wants email, SMS and eligible web push workflows together. Verify consent mapping, event freshness, country coverage and purchase exits in a pilot."
  },
  {
    "question": "When is Shopify Messaging a good fit?",
    "answer": "Consider Shopify Messaging when its native campaigns and automations cover the store’s requirements and supported audiences. Use Shopify Flow for custom workflows after checking its requirements and available actions."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do I need another email or SMS sender?",
    "answer": "No additional sender is assumed. Add a tool only for a demonstrated requirement that the selected platform cannot meet, with one owner for each message job."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can every customer receive native SMS?",
    "answer": "No. Recipient countries, opt-in, content eligibility and sender setup constrain delivery. Verify current Shopify requirements and the store’s account before enabling campaigns."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does web push replace SMS consent?",
    "answer": "No. Omnisend web push depends on browser permission and technical eligibility, including HTTPS. Email, phone and push permissions remain separate."
  },
  {
    "question": "What happens after a purchase?",
    "answer": "Recheck order truth before dispatch and retire pending promotional reminders for that order. Test delayed events and queued retries rather than assuming an automation exit cancels every pending send."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should a migration work?",
    "answer": "Assign each workflow to one sender, preserve permission history and suppressions, retire the old queue, then pilot a small cohort. Roll back by pausing new enrollment before restoring a verified owner."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Use matched eligible cohorts and a holdout. Compare incremental contribution after product, fulfillment, messaging, platform and operating costs; count discounts and returns once and use current supplier quotes."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-14">
    <nav class="mb-8"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Omnisend vs Shopify Email</nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl">
      <p class="badge">Campaign marketing comparison</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Omnisend vs Shopify Email, now Shopify Messaging</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl">Choose around the audiences you can lawfully reach, the workflows your team can operate, and the cost of a controlled campaign. Neither supplier requires a second sender by default.</p>
    </header>
    <TldrBox picks={[
      { label: 'Coordinated channels', tool: 'Omnisend', reason: 'Evaluate email, SMS and eligible web push in one operating workflow.' },
      { label: 'Native Shopify operations', tool: 'Shopify Messaging', reason: 'Evaluate native campaigns, automations and Flow against the store’s actual access and audience.' },
    ]} />
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Current product scope and access</h2>
      <p>Checked 25 November 2026. <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging">Shopify Messaging</a> is the current name for Shopify Email. It offers email and SMS marketing, with <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/requirements">store requirements</a> and channel eligibility to verify in the admin. Do not infer universal SMS access from the old product name or from installing the app.</p>
      <p><a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/2871974-create-your-omnisend-account">Omnisend</a> supports email, SMS and push workflows. Its <a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/2608314-get-started-with-push-notification-channel">web push setup</a> requires HTTPS, supported browsers and explicit browser permission; it is not universal device coverage. Check SMS destinations, sender requirements and the selected plan separately.</p>
      <p>Shopify’s <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/sms/requirements">SMS requirements</a> list supported recipient countries, with Austria and Spain currently paused. Geography can change: validate every proposed destination at launch. Its <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/sms/setup">sender setup</a> differs by region; registration and approval may be required. Confirm the actual account’s readiness before scheduling.</p>
      <p>Native <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations">marketing automations</a> include email and SMS. Shopify directs custom workflows and third-party integrations to Flow; <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/requirements-considerations">full functionality requires Flow</a>. Test the trigger, action and exit needed for your campaign rather than describing native automation as absent.</p>
      <h2>Conditional fits to test</h2>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Omnisend</th><th>Shopify Messaging</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Campaign ownership</td><td>Useful candidate for one team coordinating email, SMS and eligible push.</td><td>Useful candidate for a team operating campaigns within Shopify.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Workflow depth</td><td>Pilot channel branching, synced events and suppression behavior.</td><td>Pilot native automations and required Flow actions with actual permissions.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Audience coverage</td><td>Verify destinations, channel permissions and technical push eligibility.</td><td>Verify store access, supported destinations and approved sender setup.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Commercial fit</td><td>Quote the required contact and send mix with peak usage.</td><td>Quote the same audience, message mix and operating work.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h2>Fourteen proposed fictional merchant fixtures</h2>
      <p>These are distinct planning examples, not customers, measured results or supplier endorsements. Each fixture must pass the same permission, inventory and purchase-exit tests in either candidate.</p>
      <h3>Patchwork Denim: one-of-one denim</h3>
      <p>Route new listings by declared style and available size; remove sold items before dispatch.</p>
      <h3>Arena Energy: sports nutrition</h3>
      <p>Use a flavor-drop text only for the matching audience; keep athlete detail and any shaker offer in email, with product eligibility reviewed.</p>
      <h3>Loom & Frame: limited art prints</h3>
      <p>Use verified edition inventory and collector preferences; do not invent scarcity or assume past spend grants SMS permission.</p>
      <h3>Driftwood Surf Co: seasonal surfwear</h3>
      <p>Coordinate forecast-themed email and restock alerts for a consented waitlist; expire an alert when its size sells out.</p>
      <h3>Noir Collective: streetwear drops</h3>
      <p>Test a scheduled VIP window against a quieter calendar; cap combined messages and stop reminders after purchase.</p>
      <h3>SoleDrop ATX: sneaker releases</h3>
      <p>Use size-specific waitlist priority with expiring access links; reconcile reservations and stock before a general release.</p>
      <h3>Festival Threads Co: tour merchandise</h3>
      <p>Match city preferences and local event deadlines; resolve recipient timezone before queueing and reject expired tour offers.</p>
      <h3>Vinyl Vault Records: collector records</h3>
      <p>Separate pressing preferences from purchase value; validate stock and avoid sending a collector duplicate access links on retry.</p>
      <h3>Bloom & Wick: candle gifts</h3>
      <p>Separate gift deadlines from self-purchase education; enforce real shipping cutoffs and hand delivery questions to support.</p>
      <h3>Peak Trail Gear: outdoor clearance</h3>
      <p>Test an offer within a bargain-sensitive cohort against a holdout; protect full-price buyers from automatic discount escalation.</p>
      <h3>Sauce Lab: small-batch hot sauce</h3>
      <p>Confirm remaining batch stock before a scarcity alert; suppress sold-out links and approve sensitive product copy.</p>
      <h3>Ember Ceramics: maker pottery</h3>
      <p>Explain the kiln collection by email and pilot consented waitlist access; preserve allocation order when a worker retries.</p>
      <h3>Ridge Run Coffee: coffee subscriptions</h3>
      <p>Reconcile active subscription status before flash offers; retire obsolete subscription and replenishment reminders.</p>
      <h3>Cipher Audio: premium headphones</h3>
      <p>Sequence product education and an eligible launch reminder; compare a no-discount proposal with a matched offer test without claiming a margin result.</p>
      <h2>Permission, identity and order truth</h2>
      <p>Retain channel-specific opt-in source, timestamp, disclosure version, scope and revocation history. An email address, a Shopify customer record or an order does not establish phone marketing permission. Resolve customer IDs, normalized phone numbers and email addresses without merging unrelated household members. Preserve suppressions through imports; quarantine ambiguous identities and unknown permission states.</p>
      <p>Use Shopify order and inventory records as the purchase and availability authority, recording event time as well as receipt time. Test partial purchases, cancellations, refunds and delayed order events. Recheck current consent, product availability, offer expiry and recent purchase immediately before dispatch. A cancellation or refund must not silently re-enroll someone into a promotional sequence.</p>
      <h2>Queue retirement, approvals and support</h2>
      <p>Assign one owner to each audience, campaign and message job. Purchase exits must retire queued reminders and scheduled retries as well as stop new enrollment. Use a stable campaign/customer/step key for retry deduplication and retain dispatch acknowledgments; reconcile uncertain delivery before resending. Test out-of-order and duplicate events and a purchase between scheduling and dispatch. Document any provider queue that cannot be canceled and block launch until its exposure is understood.</p>
      <p>Require an operator to approve the audience snapshot, inventory, incentive, copy, destination eligibility and quiet-hour handling before release. Use recipient-local scheduling and current applicable requirements rather than a universal legal time window. Pause unknown timezones for review. Test opt-out processing and the sender’s actual reply capabilities. Shopify’s toll-free setup documentation says customer replies cannot be received at that number: provide a monitored support link or contact route, and name the person handling order questions and escalation.</p>
      <p>For a handoff, export permission and suppression records, map event and workflow ownership, freeze old enrollment and retire old queues before enabling the replacement. Reconcile scheduled jobs and dispatch logs on both sides. Roll back by pausing new sends, preserving fresh opt-outs and purchases, and restoring only the verified prior workflow owner. Never replay a stale queue simply because an import completed.</p>
      <h2>Matched hypothetical economics</h2>
      <p>Use randomly matched eligible customers, the same offer, observation window, inventory and country mix, plus a holdout. Compare incremental contribution per eligible customer rather than platform-attributed sales or per-send revenue alone. Report message volume and delivery separately so a quieter option is not penalized for sending less.</p>
      <p>Illustrative arithmetic only: for either candidate, suppose net sales after discounts and returns are 1,000 currency units, product and fulfillment costs are 400, messaging costs are 50, and allocated platform plus operating costs are 100. Contribution is 1,000 − 400 − 50 − 100 = 450. Against a matched holdout contribution of 350, incremental contribution is 100; for 1,000 eligible customers it is 0.10 each. These are hypothetical inputs, not prices, forecasts or merchant outcomes. Do not subtract discounts or returns again after net sales.</p>
      <p>Replace every input with current quotes, carrier or regional charges, message segmentation, peak usage, setup labor and observed support cost. Include Flow or integration work where needed. Keep the cohort and allocation method identical across suppliers. See <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a>.</p>
      <h2>Decision after the pilot</h2>
      <p>Choose Omnisend if its coordinated channels meet the required workflow and the team can operate them reliably. Choose Shopify Messaging if native campaigns and automations with the needed Flow actions meet the same acceptance criteria for the store’s eligible audience. Keep either choice conditional on purchase exits, queue behavior, permission preservation and matched economics. If a requirement fails, document the gap before adding or replacing a sender.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Omnisend vs Shopify Email FAQ" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
